task: jdk-event-time-collector
agent: opus-flink
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/jdk-event-time-collector
worktree: ../okay-wt-jdk-windows
spec: specs/event-time-windows.md (Windows.absorb), docs/benchmarks.md §20

why: the operator's question after §20's third-engine table — "можешь
  исправить что-то для jdk streams? добавить туда event time?" The
  lane's OutOfMemoryError is not `java.util.stream`'s fault and not
  ours-in-general: it is `Collectors.groupingBy`, which has no notion
  of a group being COMPLETE, so every pane of the run stays live. The
  collector is our side of the seam, and an okay `Aggregator` is
  already a `Collector` — so an event-time WINDOW can be one too.

what: `okay.Windows.absorb(other)(emit)` in the core — merge another
  operator's panes into this one, dropping what is already behind this
  watermark (the operator's own late rule, kept) and sweeping after.
  Then `okay.java.Windowed.collector(...)`: a `Collector` whose
  accumulator is a `Windows` plus a downstream aggregator's state,
  folding each pane as it CLOSES and evicting it. Sequential it is
  plainly right; parallel it is right for the same reason the
  merge-parallel okay lane is — on an ordered stream the combiner sees
  (left, right) in encounter order, and a pane left has closed cannot
  receive an element from right unless out-of-orderness exceeds
  `lateness`, which is exactly what the watermark asserts. Plus the
  bunching stage as an Aggregator (first/last/gap per key, merged
  across the seam), so the lane holds one accumulator per key instead
  of every element.

predict: the JDK lane finishes the FULL 2.4M feed on the heap where it
  used to die, and the parallel penalty shrinks — the per-split maps
  that were the work become small. If parallel is still slower than
  sequential, that is the shuffle-free JDK's own shape and gets
  reported as such.
